{#
  Cloudflare Web Analytics.

  The site shipped with no measurement of any kind, which meant there was no
  answer to the only question Ava will ever ask about it: is it bringing me
  work. This is the smallest thing that answers it — visits, referrers and
  which pages get read.

  Cookieless and aggregated on Cloudflare's side. It sets nothing on her
  visitors' devices and stores nothing per person, which is why there is no
  consent banner here and why this does not contradict the two decisions
  already made on the same grounds: the Google Maps iframe was kept off the
  page because it is ~800KB and sets cookies. A beacon that does neither is a
  different trade.

  What it cannot do is tell you who called. Most of her conversions are a tap
  on a tel: link and those leave from the page without a pageview behind them,
  so treat these numbers as traffic, not leads.

  THE TOKEN LIVES HERE, NOT IN site.json, and that is deliberate. The admin
  panel writes _data/site.json back with only the fields declared in
  admin/config.yml, so a key it has never heard of is dropped the first time
  Ava saves her hours — the beacon would go quiet weeks later with nothing to
  point at. _data/analytics.json is not a CMS collection and nothing but a
  deploy can touch it.

  The token is public by design; it identifies the site to Cloudflare and is
  visible in the HTML of every page. It is not a credential and does not need
  hiding. Get it from Cloudflare's dashboard under Web Analytics, "Add a site",
  attentiontodetailwa.com.

  Empty token renders nothing at all, so a build before the token exists ships
  a clean page rather than a beacon posting to nowhere.

  `defer` and last in the body: this is the least important request on the
  page and it should never be in front of a photo of a car.
#}
{%- if analytics.cloudflareToken %}
<script defer src="https://static.cloudflareinsights.com/beacon.min.js" data-cf-beacon='{"token": "{{ analytics.cloudflareToken }}"}'></script>
{%- endif -%}
